      "text": "1. The equation of an ellipse with a major axis of length \\(2a\\) and a minor axis of length \\(2b\\) centered at the origin is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n   \\]\n   Here, the lengths of the major and minor axes are 10 and 8, respectively. Thus, \\(a = 5\\) and \\(b = 4\\). Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^2}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. The foci of the ellipse are located at \\((\\pm c, 0)\\) where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Using \\(a = 5\\) and \\(b = 4\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   c = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the foci are at \\((3, 0)\\) and \\((-3, 0)\\).\n\n3. Consider a circle centered at one of the foci, say \\((3, 0)\\), that is tangent to the ellipse. The radius \\(r\\) of this circle will be equal to the distance from the focus to the point of tangency on the ellipse.\n\n4. Let's find the point of tangency \\(P\\) on the ellipse. The point \\(P\\) lies on the ellipse and is also on the line passing through the foci \\((3, 0)\\) and having a perpendicular distance equal to the radius \\(r\\).\n\n5. The line through the focus \\((3, 0)\\) is \\(x = 3\\). To find the point of intersection of this line with the ellipse, substitute \\(x = 3\\) into the ellipse equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3^2}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{9}{25} + \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y^2}{16} = 1 - \\frac{9}{25} = \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{9}{25} = \\frac{16}{25}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y^2 = 16 \\cdot \\frac{16}{25} = \\frac{256}{25}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\pm \\frac{16}{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. The points of tangency are \\(\\left(3, \\frac{16}{5}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(3, -\\frac{16}{5}\\right)\\).\n\n7. The distance from the focus \\((3, 0)\\) to the point of tangency \\(\\left(3, \\frac{16}{5}\\right)\\) is the radius \\(r\\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{(3 - 3)^2 + \\left(\\frac{16}{5} - 0\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{0 + \\left(\\frac{16}{5}\\right)^2} = \\frac{16}{5}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{16}{5}}\\).",
