      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle that is tangent to the ellipse with one focus as the center, we need to understand the relationship between the ellipse and the circle. Let's denote the semi-major axis by \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis by \\(b\\). The length of the major axis is 10, so \\(a = 5\\). The length of the minor axis is 8, so \\(b = 4\\).\n\nThe distance between the center of the ellipse and each focus (denoted by \\(c\\)) is given by the equation:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3. \\]\nThus, the foci of the ellipse are at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\nThe circle is centered at one of the foci, say \\((3, 0)\\), and is tangent to the ellipse. The radius of the circle is the distance from the center of the circle to the ellipse, which is the distance from \\((3, 0)\\) to the point where the circle is tangent to the ellipse.\n\nSince the circle is tangent to the ellipse and no part of the circle is outside the ellipse, the radius of the circle is equal to the distance from the center of the ellipse to the point of tangency, which is the distance from the center of the ellipse to the focus minus the semi-minor axis.\n\nThe distance from the center of the ellipse at \\((0, 0)\\) to the focus at \\((3, 0)\\) is 3, which is the length of the semi-major axis minus the length of the semi-minor axis:\n\\[ \\text{Distance from center to focus} = a - b = 5 - 4 = 1. \\]\nHowever, since the ellipse is elongated and the circle is tangent to the ellipse, the radius of the circle is actually the distance from the focus to the point of tangency, which is equal to the length of the semi-major axis minus the distance from the center of the ellipse to the focus:\n\\[ \\text{Radius of the circle} = a - c = 5 - 3 = 2. \\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
