      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle inscribed in the ellipse, we start by recalling the equation of the ellipse and its properties. The standard form of an ellipse centered at the origin with major axis \\(2a\\) and minor axis \\(2b\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nGiven that the lengths of the major and minor axes are 10 and 8 respectively, we have \\(2a = 10\\) and \\(2b = 8\\). Therefore, \\(a = 5\\) and \\(b = 4\\). The distance from the center to each focus is given by \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\\). So, the foci of the ellipse are at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\nThe key insight is that the circle is tangent to the ellipse at exactly one point on each of the major and minor axes. The radius of this circle is equal to the distance from the center of the ellipse to the point of tangency on the ellipse along a line perpendicular to the major or minor axis. This distance is given by \\(c\\), because the circle is tangent to the ellipse from the inside, and the distance from the center to the focus is \\(c\\), and the radius of the circle is the same as the distance from the center to the point of tangency.\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
